title: AWS Security
page: aws-security.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: AWS owns the physical layer and the hypervisor; you own the configuration, the identity, the data and almost every misconfiguration the attacker exploits.

== The 30-second version
layout: text
section: top
The platform is mature and its failure modes are well known: IAM too broad, S3 buckets too public, IMDS too easy to abuse, CloudTrail missing in some region. Almost every misconfiguration an attacker exploits sits on your side.
notes:
The page notes that most people who search for cloud security mean AWS security, because AWS is the cloud they run. The platform is mature, the primitives are well documented, and the failure modes are well known: IAM that is too broad, S3 buckets that are too public, IMDS that is too easy to abuse, and CloudTrail that is missing in some region.

If you remember one thing, the page says, remember that the shared responsibility model moves the line. AWS owns the physical layer and the hypervisor. You own the configuration, the identity, the data, and almost every misconfiguration the attacker exploits. Build the muscle first, then let the certifications and the CSPM follow.

This deck follows the page: the Well-Architected Security Pillar, the service catalog, a reference landing zone, the top 10 misconfigurations, the attack paths AWS specifically enables, and how native tools compare with third-party ones.

== Same disciplines, different controls
layout: compare
section: why-aws
col: What breaches AWS | Public S3 | IMDSv1 abuse | Wildcard Resource: *
col: What breaches Azure | Over-privileged service principals | Public storage accounts | Exposed managed identities
The disciplines rhyme across providers, but the controls don't translate one-to-one. Each cloud has its own misconfigurations that breach it.
notes:
Cloud security exists as a category because the disciplines (identity, network, data, detection and response) rhyme across providers. The controls do not translate one-to-one. An IAM policy in AWS is a different shape from an Azure RBAC role assignment, which is a different shape from a GCP IAM binding. A GuardDuty finding triggers a different response runbook than a Defender for Cloud alert.

The misconfigurations differ too. Public S3, IMDSv1 abuse and wildcard Resource: * breach AWS environments. Over-privileged service principals, public storage accounts and exposed managed identities breach Azure environments.

The page's opinions are vendor-neutral but not provider-neutral. It credits AWS for IAM expressiveness, service breadth, the partner ecosystem and deep documentation. It criticizes defaults that favor functionality over safety, naming that drifts over time, and IAM that is uniquely capable of producing surprising privilege-escalation paths.

== Shared responsibility: the line moves by service
layout: table
section: shared-responsibility
row: Service | What you own
row: EC2 | A lot: OS, patches, agent posture, security groups
row: RDS | Less: no OS, but network, IAM and encryption
row: Lambda | Only the code, the IAM role and a few runtime knobs
row: S3 | Only the configuration and the data
AWS owns security of the cloud; you own security in the cloud. On S3, the configuration and the data account for most S3 breaches.
notes:
AWS owns security of the cloud: the hardware, the hypervisor, the physical data center, the network backbone, and the parts of managed services you cannot see. You own security in the cloud: your identities, your network configuration, your OS and application patching where applicable, your data and its encryption, and the configuration of every service you turn on.

The line shifts by service, as the table shows. On S3 you own only the configuration and the data, but those two account for most S3 breaches.

The page draws two practical implications. First, every audit conversation begins with you handing the auditor AWS's SOC 2, ISO 27001 and PCI DSS reports, downloaded from AWS Artifact, to cover AWS's half. Then you produce your own evidence for your half: CloudTrail, Config, Security Hub findings and IAM exports. Second, almost every cloud breach you've read about happened on the customer's side of the line. AWS having FedRAMP High doesn't make your S3 bucket private.

== The Security Pillar's seven design principles
layout: checklist
section: well-architected
check: Implement a strong identity foundation
check: Maintain traceability
check: Apply security at all layers
check: Automate security best practices
check: Protect data in transit and at rest
check: Keep people away from data
check: Prepare for security events
The page calls them mostly common sense, and consistently the right answer when teams disagree.
notes:
The AWS Well-Architected Framework codifies what AWS thinks good looks like, and its Security Pillar lays out seven design principles.

None of them are surprising. Their value is that they are documented, prioritized and periodically revised by AWS. So when an engineering team and a security team disagree on direction, asking what the Well-Architected Security Pillar says is a productive question.

The next two slides show what each principle means in practice on AWS.

== Principles 1 and 2 in practice
layout: compare
section: well-architected
col: Identity foundation | Identity Center, or your IdP federated through it | No long-lived IAM users for humans | Least privilege via policies, boundaries, SCPs | Access Analyzer for unintended external access
col: Traceability | CloudTrail in all regions, org trail to log archive | VPC Flow Logs, ALB and CloudFront logs | Data events for sensitive S3 buckets | Security Hub, then a SIEM
Identity is the only principle that touches every other pillar. The first 24 hours of an incident run on forensic-quality logs, or on nothing.
notes:
A strong identity foundation means centralizing identity through IAM Identity Center, or your identity provider federated through it. Eliminate long-lived IAM users for humans. Enforce least privilege through IAM policies, permission boundaries and Service Control Policies, and use IAM Access Analyzer to surface unintended external access. The page says the identity foundation is the only one that touches every other pillar.

Traceability means CloudTrail turned on in all regions, with an organization trail centralized to a log-archive account. Enable VPC Flow Logs, ALB and CloudFront access logs, and CloudTrail data events for sensitive S3 buckets. Aggregate to Security Hub and ship to a SIEM.

The page's point about logs is blunt: the first 24 hours of an incident is forensic-quality logs, or it's nothing.

== Principles 3 to 7 in practice
layout: table
section: well-architected
row: Principle | In practice
row: Security at all layers | SCPs, VPC edge, host, workload and data controls
row: Automate | Findings route to EventBridge; preventive controls ship as code
row: Protect data | KMS by default, TLS 1.2+, Block Public Access, Macie
row: Keep people away from data | Query interfaces, redacted exports, Session Manager
row: Prepare for events | IR runbook, game-days, a pre-staged forensics account
Each layer assumes the one above will fail, and manual security at scale is no security.
notes:
Defense in depth puts SCPs at the org perimeter, network controls at the VPC edge, host controls inside the VPC, application controls in the workload, and data controls under the workload. Single-layer security is single-point-of-failure security.

Automation means detective controls such as Security Hub, Config rules and GuardDuty findings should auto-route to EventBridge, into either ticketing or auto-remediation Lambdas. Preventive controls such as SCPs, permission boundaries and Config conformance packs should ship as code.

For data, encrypt by default through KMS-managed keys, and reach for customer-managed keys where compliance, key rotation or cross-account access policy demands it. Use TLS 1.2+ at every public edge through ACM-issued certificates, S3 Block Public Access at the account level, and Macie for sensitive-data discovery. The page says the encryption is easy and the key-management story is the work.

Humans should not have direct production-data access. Build query interfaces, redacted exports and break-glass workflows that produce audit logs, and use Session Manager rather than SSH bastions for any necessary host access.

To prepare, have an IR runbook and run game-days against it, because the runbook you've never executed will not work the first time. Pre-stage IR tooling in a forensics account with EBS-snapshot copy permissions. Practice the chain: detection in GuardDuty or Security Hub, triage in Detective, evidence collection via API, and containment via SCPs or quarantine VPCs.

== Detection services
layout: table
section: services
row: Service | What it does
row: GuardDuty | Managed threat detection; the first detective control to turn on
row: Security Hub CSPM | Findings aggregator and CSPM, running CIS, PCI and NIST standards
row: Security Hub | Correlated exposure findings with potential attack paths
row: Detective | Investigation graph for an incident's timeline
row: Inspector | CVE-based scanning for EC2, ECR images and Lambda
row: Macie | Sensitive-data discovery for S3, in targeted scans
Security Hub CSPM is the service AWS called Security Hub until it split the name in two. The new Security Hub needs it for posture findings.
notes:
The page tours the catalog by function rather than by console category, saying what each service does, when it earns its keep and where it falls short.

GuardDuty reads CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, DNS logs, EKS audit logs, S3 events, RDS login activity, EC2 runtime with the agent, and Lambda runtime. The page calls it the first detective control to turn on in every account: findings are high signal and the noise is manageable. Turn it on org-wide with the delegated-admin pattern.

Security Hub CSPM pulls from GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, IAM Access Analyzer, Config and dozens of partners, runs CIS, PCI, NIST and AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standards, and pushes to EventBridge for routing. It is a single pane of glass for AWS-native findings, and not a substitute for a third-party CNAPP if you are multi-cloud.

The separate Security Hub, generally available since December 2025, correlates Security Hub CSPM, GuardDuty, Inspector and Macie findings into exposure findings, each with a potential attack path and blast radius diagram. It needs Security Hub CSPM enabled to see posture findings, AWS recommends running both, and it covers AWS only.

Detective earns its keep during an incident; spend less time in it day to day. Inspector is a good baseline, though the page says a third-party scanner tends to outperform it on container depth. Macie's per-GB pricing makes scanning everything expensive, so target the data classifications you care about.

== CloudTrail and AWS Config
layout: compare
section: services
col: CloudTrail | The audit log of the AWS control plane | Management events free; data events paid | Org trail to a log-archive account | Most breach investigations begin and end here
col: AWS Config | Resource-configuration history and rule evaluation | The substrate most Security Hub CSPM controls depend on | On org-wide, aggregated to a central account | Cost grows with resources and rules
The page calls an org trail to a log-archive account the only correct CloudTrail configuration.
notes:
CloudTrail is the audit log of the AWS control plane. Management events are free. Data events for S3, Lambda and DynamoDB are paid. The page says an org trail to a log-archive account is the only correct configuration, and that most cloud breach investigations begin and end in CloudTrail.

AWS Config records resource-configuration history and evaluates rules, with managed and custom rules and conformance packs for frameworks. Most Security Hub CSPM controls depend on it. Turn it on org-wide and aggregate to a central account. Its cost grows with resource count and rule count, so review it periodically.

== Identity services
layout: table
section: services
row: Service | What it does
row: IAM | Per-account users, roles and policies; uniquely expressive
row: IAM Identity Center | Workforce identity with short-lived role sessions
row: IAM Access Analyzer | Free; finds external, public and unused access
row: IAM Roles Anywhere | X.509 certificates for workloads outside AWS
row: Cognito | Customer identity for B2C apps, not workforce
row: RAM | Shares resources across accounts; audit the sharing
IAM is uniquely capable of producing surprising privilege-escalation paths, and almost every AWS breach involves an IAM misuse. Use Identity Center for all human access.
notes:
IAM is the per-account identity service: users, groups, roles, policies, permission boundaries and condition keys. The page calls it uniquely expressive and uniquely capable of producing surprising privilege-escalation paths.

IAM Identity Center, formerly AWS SSO, federates your identity provider (Okta, Entra ID, Google Workspace or its own directory) into AWS Organizations and brokers short-lived role sessions across accounts. The page calls it the right answer for all human access in 2026.

IAM Access Analyzer is free. It surfaces S3 buckets, KMS keys, IAM roles, Lambda functions, SQS queues and Secrets Manager secrets shared with external accounts or the public, plus unused access findings and policy validation. Turn it on org-wide and review the public and external findings weekly.

IAM Roles Anywhere lets workloads on-prem, in other clouds or in SaaS authenticate with X.509 certificates and assume IAM roles. It replaces hard-coded AWS access keys on non-AWS hosts.

Cognito is fine for B2C apps, but not for workforce identity or fine-grained authorization. Resource Access Manager shares resources such as VPC subnets and Transit Gateway attachments within an Organization. The page calls it underused, and warns that over-sharing is a real risk.

== Data services
layout: table
section: services
row: Service | What it does
row: KMS | The crypto API; most teams under-invest in key policy
row: CloudHSM | FIPS 140-2 Level 3 hardware for specific regulations
row: Secrets Manager | Auto-rotated secrets, instead of env-var secrets
row: ACM | Free, auto-renewed public TLS certificates; Private CA
row: S3 Block Public Access | On at the account level, on day one
row: S3 Object Lock | WORM protection for ransomware-resistant backups
The page calls account-level Block Public Access the most impactful single click in AWS security.
notes:
KMS offers AWS-managed keys, free and per service, customer-managed keys at $1/month plus API calls, and external or XKS keys. The key policy is the access-control surface most teams under-invest in. Rotate yearly, restrict kms:Decrypt with condition keys, and alarm on key deletion.

CloudHSM is needed for very specific regulatory requirements, such as some payment-card flows. For almost all customers KMS is the right answer, and the page says not to over-engineer here.

Secrets Manager gives centralized storage with automatic rotation, KMS-backed encryption and IAM-controlled access. Use it instead of environment-variable secrets in Lambda or container task definitions, with Parameter Store for non-secret config.

ACM issues free public certificates for ALB, NLB, CloudFront, API Gateway and other endpoints. Treat the private CA hierarchy with the gravity of any root of trust.

S3 Block Public Access overrides any public ACL or bucket policy. Turn it on at the account level on day one, then carve out the vanishingly rare buckets that need public access through OAC and CloudFront. S3 Object Lock, in Compliance or Governance mode, pairs with versioning and replication to a separate account for backups.

== Network services
layout: table
section: services
row: Service | What it does
row: Security Groups and NACLs | SGs as the primary control; NACLs for explicit denies
row: AWS WAF | Managed rule groups for ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway
row: Shield | Standard free for L3/L4 DDoS; Advanced $3K/month adds L7
row: PrivateLink | VPC endpoints keep the control plane off the internet
row: Network Firewall | Suricata-compatible rules at VPC egress, per-GB pricing
row: DNS Firewall | Blocks known-bad domains; cheap and high-signal
0.0.0.0/0 on SSH or RDP is a finding, not a configuration.
notes:
The VPC is the network primitive: design it once and change it reluctantly, because every workload depends on it. Security Groups are stateful, instance-level and allow-only. NACLs are stateless, subnet-level, and allow and deny. Use Security Groups as the primary control and reach for NACLs only when you need an explicit deny at the subnet boundary.

AWS WAF covers ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway, App Runner and Cognito user pools with AWS-managed and Marketplace rule groups. The page calls it a good baseline, and says that for high-volume apps or sophisticated bot management, Cloudflare and Akamai are typically deeper.

Shield Standard is free and automatic for all AWS customers. Advanced adds L7 protection, the DDoS Response Team and cost-protection credits, and the page says it is worth the spend only for high-profile or revenue-critical public surfaces.

PrivateLink and VPC endpoints keep the control plane off the internet; the page calls them cheap and high-impact. Network Firewall earns its keep for egress filtering, IDS and IPS on north-south traffic, and TLS-SNI blocklisting, but per-GB pricing means thinking hard about scope. Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall catches malware C2, accidental exfiltration to file-share domains, and the long tail of traffic you should not be sending.

VPC Lattice is worth piloting for service-mesh use cases that don't need a full service mesh. The page calls Network Firewall and Cloud WAN enterprise patterns that small shops over-buy.

== Governance and incident response
layout: table
section: services
row: Service | What it does
row: Organizations and SCPs | Org-perimeter limits over member-account IAM
row: Control Tower | Default starting point for new deployments in 2026
row: AWS Artifact | AWS's compliance reports for the provider half
row: Audit Manager | Framework-mapped evidence for AWS-centric programs
row: Incident Manager | Closed to new customers since 7 November 2025
row: IR runbook library | AWS playbooks on GitHub to fork and game-day
The page calls SCPs the strongest preventive control in AWS; use them sparingly and explicitly.
notes:
Organizations is the account-grouping layer. SCPs are the org-perimeter preventive control: they constrain what IAM principals in member accounts can do, no matter what their IAM policies say.

Control Tower deploys Organizations, OUs, Identity Center, log-archive and audit accounts, an org-wide CloudTrail, a Config aggregator, and a starter set of preventive and detective guardrails.

AWS Artifact is the self-serve portal for AWS's compliance reports, such as SOC, ISO 27001, PCI DSS and FedRAMP. It is the first stop in every auditor conversation. Audit Manager pulls evidence on a schedule and maps it to frameworks; it pairs poorly with multi-cloud GRC, where you would want a dedicated platform.

During an incident, Detective is the next page after a GuardDuty finding triggers a ticket, and Security Hub is the findings aggregator and triage console. Pre-build your insights and saved searches before you need them. Systems Manager Incident Manager applies only to accounts that already have it enabled. AWS's customer IR playbook framework has prebuilt runbooks for credential exposure, IAM compromise and S3 exposure.

== A multi-account landing zone
layout: figure
section: landing-zone
figure: aws-accounts
Past three accounts, the page's starting shape is a landing zone built on Organizations, Control Tower and Identity Center.
notes:
The figure shows the account roles in a landing zone: management, log archive, audit, identity, network, workload and sandbox. As the caption says, nothing but the organization runs in the management account.

The page calls this the pattern that almost every well-run AWS estate converges on, regardless of whether they started there. The landing zones page covers the building blocks in depth.

== What each account does
layout: table
section: landing-zone
row: Account | Role
row: Management | Organizations, SCPs, billing; nothing else runs here
row: Log archive | Org trail, Config, access logs; object-locked, versioned
row: Audit / security tooling | Delegated admin for the security services
row: Identity Center | Federation to the corporate IdP; permission sets
row: Network / shared services | Transit Gateway, Network Firewall, private DNS
row: Workload | One per team or application, per environment
Sandbox accounts are disposable, time-boxed and auto-cleaned, which keeps engineers out of production accounts. Control Tower deploys most of this in an afternoon.
notes:
The management account is the root of the AWS Organization. Its root account is hardened, and humans don't log in to it day to day.

The log archive account receives the org-wide CloudTrail, the Config aggregator, ALB, CloudFront and VPC Flow logs, and S3 access logs. Limited principals can read it and almost none can write.

The audit or security tooling account holds the delegated admins, third-party CNAPP read-only roles and the SIEM ingestion role. The security team operates from here. Identity Center assignments roll out across all accounts from the identity account.

The network account also holds Route 53 private hosted zones and AD Connector or Managed Microsoft AD. Workload accounts connect through RAM-shared subnets or their own VPC peered through Transit Gateway; SCPs prevent destructive actions, and CloudTrail and Config send to log archive. Sandbox accounts get generous SCPs that deny only the truly destructive.

The AWS Landing Zone Accelerator is the CDK and CloudFormation alternative for environments that need bespoke control sets Control Tower can't express.

== Top 10 misconfigurations: the first five
layout: table
section: misconfigurations
row: Misconfiguration | Mitigation
row: Public S3 buckets | Account-level Block Public Access, Access Analyzer, SCPs
row: IMDSv1 still enabled | Enforce IMDSv2: account setting, launch templates, Config rule
row: No MFA on the root account | Hardware MFA, vaulted password, root-login alerts
row: Wildcard IAM policies | Permission boundaries, policy validation, reviews
row: Public RDS snapshots | SCP deny, Config rule, Access Analyzer scans
Drawn from the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark, AWS Foundational Security Best Practices and public breach post-mortems, roughly in order of how often each shows up in incidents.
notes:
Public S3 buckets are still the canonical AWS breach. The fix is a single click; the audit ritual is to verify it's clicked.

IMDSv1 is fetchable via blind SSRF, and SSRFs in cloud workloads are a top finding. IMDSv2 requires a PUT to get a session token before the GET.

Root has unrestricted access, and MFA is the only credential-theft protection. Root MFA is CIS check 1.4, and the page says it should never fail.

Wildcard policies mean Action: * or Resource: *, especially in custom policies, though AWS managed policies are not automatically narrow: AdministratorAccess is Action: * on Resource: *. Action: * on a role used by Lambda is a privilege escalation away from total account compromise. Least-privilege reviews use CloudTrail-derived access reports.

An RDS snapshot shared publicly exposes the entire database. The SCP denies rds:ModifyDBSnapshotAttribute with public, and IAM Access Analyzer's RDS coverage gives a weekly scan.

== Top 10 misconfigurations: the last five
layout: table
section: misconfigurations
row: Misconfiguration | Mitigation
row: Access keys in public repositories | Eliminate long-lived keys; secret scanning
row: SSH or RDP open to 0.0.0.0/0 | Session Manager, or a fixed source range
row: Default VPC in production | Delete it in every region of every account
row: CloudTrail not in all regions | Org trail on all regions, log-file validation
row: Root used for daily operations | Identity Center for humans; alarm on every root sign-in
Each is a single line in a Security Hub or CSPM dashboard. They keep showing up because defaults favor functionality, org-level controls are opt-in, and turnover leaves new accounts without central guardrails.
notes:
Long-lived IAM user keys committed to GitHub get scraped within minutes, and AWS's own scanners catch many, but not all. Eliminate long-lived keys: Identity Center for humans, IAM roles for workloads, Roles Anywhere for non-AWS workloads. Add GitHub secret scanning, and GitGuardian or TruffleHog in CI.

Open SSH and RDP draw endless scanning. Better still, the page says, don't SSH at all.

The default VPC has overly permissive defaults, an internet gateway and a default security group allowing internal traffic. Deleting it forces teams to think about networking when they create one.

CloudTrail also needs management events enabled. Attackers pivot to disabled regions, so the trail must cover all regions, including future ones, and deliver to the log-archive account.

Daily use of root means there's no IAM model.

All ten are trivially detectable, and they keep showing up anyway, because turnover means new accounts get spun up without the central guardrails until somebody notices.

== IMDS abuse via SSRF
layout: compare
section: attack-paths
col: IMDSv1 | An SSRF reaches the Instance Metadata Service | It reads the instance role's temporary credentials | Works from workloads on EC2, ECS or EKS
col: IMDSv2 | Requires a session token via PUT first | Most SSRF primitives can't make that PUT
Capital One (2019) ran this exact chain: an SSRF through a misconfigured WAF read IMDSv1 credentials and exfiltrated 100M+ customer records from S3. Enforce IMDSv2 everywhere.
notes:
The page's attack paths are the ones AWS specifically enables. They are not novel, and many derive from generic SSRF, supply-chain or credential-theft chains, but the AWS-specific mechanic is the part worth knowing.

A server-side request forgery in a workload running on EC2, ECS or EKS pivots to the Instance Metadata Service and reads the instance role's temporary credentials. IMDSv2 mitigates this by requiring a session token via PUT, which most SSRF primitives can't make. So enforce IMDSv2 everywhere.

The real-world example is the Capital One breach in 2019, which ran this exact chain: an SSRF through a misconfigured WAF read IMDSv1 credentials and exfiltrated 100M+ customer records from S3.

== Attack paths through IAM
layout: table
section: attack-paths
row: Path | Mitigation
row: Privilege escalation chains | Permission boundaries, unused-access findings, reviews
row: AssumeRole pivoting across accounts | Review external-trust policies Access Analyzer finds
row: iam:PassRole abuse | Constrain with iam:PassedToService and resource ARNs
Permissions like iam:CreateAccessKey on another user, or iam:PassRole plus lambda:CreateFunction, let an attacker with limited access escalate. Tools like Pacu and PMapper enumerate these chains.
notes:
Permissions like iam:CreateAccessKey on another user, iam:AttachRolePolicy on a role you can assume, iam:PassRole plus lambda:CreateFunction, and a long list of others let an attacker with limited initial access escalate.

AssumeRole pivoting works when a trust policy allows a too-broad source, such as arn:aws:iam::*:root, or doesn't constrain sts:ExternalId for vendor cross-account roles. Then accounts you don't control can assume the role.

With iam:PassRole, an attacker who can pass a more-privileged role to a service such as Lambda, EC2, ECS, Glue or SageMaker escalates by creating a resource that runs as that role.

== Attack paths through secrets, snapshots, SSM and logs
layout: table
section: attack-paths
row: Path | Mitigation
row: Lambda env-var secrets | Secrets Manager or Parameter Store, fetched at runtime
row: EBS direct API exfil | SCP restriction, alerts on the API calls
row: SSM RunCommand abuse | Tight SSM permissions, alerts, SSM logs to the SIEM
row: CloudTrail tampering | Separate log-archive trail, validation, Object Lock, alerts
EBS direct API reads don't trigger common detections. An attacker with ssm:SendCommand runs commands as root without opening an SSH port.
notes:
Secrets in Lambda environment variables are readable by anyone with lambda:GetFunctionConfiguration, a much broader permission than expected. Fetch them at runtime instead, and restrict the function's KMS key policy.

The EBS direct APIs, ListSnapshotBlocks and GetSnapshotBlock, let a sufficiently privileged principal read snapshot data without ever attaching a volume, and the calls don't trigger common detections. Restrict them via SCP, alert on the API calls, and review snapshot-sharing policies.

An attacker with ssm:SendCommand on EC2 instances executes commands as root without ever opening an SSH port, and the forensic trail is in SSM rather than syslog.

A compromised admin can stop logging, delete trails or modify retention. The mitigations are an org trail in a separate log-archive account, log-file validation, S3 Object Lock on the trail bucket, and alerting on trail-modification API calls.

== Threat detection across three clouds
layout: table
section: comparisons
row: Dimension | GuardDuty | Defender for Cloud | Security Command Center
row: Posture | Separate: Security Hub CSPM | Bundled: Defender CSPM | Bundled: SCC Premium
row: Attack paths | Security Hub, AWS only | In Defender CSPM | SCC Premium simulations
row: Multi-cloud | AWS only | AWS and GCP | Enterprise tier, ending 21 May 2027
row: When it wins | Single-cloud AWS shops disciplined about tuning | Microsoft-heavy enterprises | GCP-first shops
The page calls its comparison tables deliberately opinionated: calibrate them against your own environment.
notes:
The page says the right tool is the one that fits your shape, and that it is rarely AWS-only or third-party-only.

This table compares the native threat detection services. On pricing, GuardDuty charges per GB of analyzed logs plus per million events, Defender for Cloud per resource per month in plan tiers, and Security Command Center's Premium tier per asset. GuardDuty covers CloudTrail, VPC Flow, DNS, EKS, S3, RDS, Lambda and EC2 runtime. Defender for Cloud covers VMs, containers, storage, databases, App Service, key vault, DNS, APIs and AI. Security Command Center covers VMs, containers, IAM and storage, and the page calls it weaker on network.

The page also compares AWS WAF with Cloudflare and Akamai. AWS WAF adds little latency if the origin is AWS, and wins for AWS-only public surfaces and cost-sensitive teams. And it compares Inspector with two third-party scanners, where Inspector wins for cost-sensitive AWS-only shops.

== Native or third-party?
layout: table
section: native-vs-third-party
row: Capability | Native usually wins | Third-party usually wins
row: Attack-path graph | Security Hub, AWS only | CNAPP graph, across clouds
row: Framework mappings | Security Hub CSPM, Config packs | CNAPP, deeper mappings
row: GRC platform | Audit Manager, AWS-only programs | Vanta, Drata, OneTrust
row: Secrets | Secrets Manager, AWS workloads | Vault, Doppler, 1Password, hybrid
row: WAF | AWS WAF, AWS-only surfaces | Cloudflare or Akamai, perf and bots
row: DDoS | Shield Standard, free | Shield Advanced or third-party, high-risk
Start native, and add a third-party tool when you outgrow native or go multi-cloud.
notes:
Every AWS security program faces this question. For a single-cloud AWS environment with a small team, the page's native stack is Security Hub, Security Hub CSPM, GuardDuty, Inspector and Macie. For multi-cloud, Kubernetes or container depth, and SIEM, the page's table lists only third-party tools.

The bias, in the page's words, should be to start native, especially for detective controls in a single-cloud AWS environment, and earn the right to add a third-party tool when you have outgrown what native provides or gone multi-cloud.

The cost of operating an over-stack of tools is real: license, integration, maintenance and operator fatigue. The page says having everything but using none of it well is consistently worse than having less and using it well.

== The certification ladder
layout: table
section: certifications
row: Certification | Exam | What it gives you
row: Cloud Practitioner | CLF-C02 | Vocabulary; skip after a year in AWS
row: Solutions Architect Associate | SAA-C03 | Intuition the security exam assumes
row: Security Specialty | SCS-C03 | The deep end; most cloud security job descriptions list it
row: Solutions Architect Professional | SAP-C02 | Optional; multi-account, enterprise patterns
row: Advanced Networking Specialty | ANS-C01 | For whoever owns the network plane
Certifications organize your study and show baseline literacy, but won't get you a job by themselves. Pair them with a home lab: the exam tests recognition, and the job tests execution.
notes:
The page lays out a pragmatic ladder for a security engineer.

Cloud Practitioner is foundational vocabulary. Skip it if you've worked in AWS for a year, though it is useful for non-engineering stakeholders who need to talk to the security team. Solutions Architect Associate covers networking, storage, compute and IAM at the associate level; the page says the security exam will frustrate you without it.

Security Specialty covers IAM, KMS, detective controls, infrastructure security, and identity, data and IR. It is the certification most cloud-security-engineer job descriptions list, and the page says it moves the dial on hiring conversations.

Solutions Architect Professional is optional but valuable for staff and principal roles that design landing zones, migration strategies and cross-account network architectures. Advanced Networking suits engineers who own Transit Gateway, Direct Connect, Cloud WAN, IPAM and hybrid DNS.

For labs, the page names CloudGoat, flAWS, AWS Skill Builder and a personal sandbox account where you can break things.

== Is AWS more secure than Azure or GCP?
layout: question
section: faq
All three meet the major frameworks and run mature security programs. Where do the real differences lie, and what decides the outcome?
notes:
This is a question from the page's FAQ. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== No cloud is categorically more secure
layout: answer
section: faq
At the platform level, the differences are in defaults, service granularity and ecosystem. Security outcomes are determined by the operator, not the provider. Pick the cloud your team can run safely.
notes:
No cloud is categorically more secure than another at the platform level. All three meet the major frameworks, such as FedRAMP High, ISO 27001, PCI DSS and HIPAA, and run mature security programs.

The differences are in defaults, service granularity and ecosystem. For AWS, the page points to a service surface that brings both more capability and more sharp edges, a mature partner ecosystem, and IAM that is uniquely powerful and uniquely easy to misuse.

Security outcomes are determined by the operator, not the provider. Pick the cloud your team can run safely.

== Do I need a CSPM if I already have Security Hub?
layout: question
section: faq
AWS now has two services here, Security Hub CSPM and the separate Security Hub. Think about how many clouds you run, and how much time you have to tune.
notes:
This is the second FAQ question. Again, ask it before showing the answer.

== Native is fine for single-cloud, disciplined teams
layout: answer
section: faq
If you are single-cloud AWS and disciplined, Security Hub CSPM plus Security Hub is fine. If you run multi-cloud, you want a third-party CNAPP, with attack paths that cross cloud boundaries.
notes:
Security Hub CSPM, the service previously called Security Hub, is a credible CSPM for single-cloud AWS shops that have time to tune it. The separate Security Hub, generally available since December 2025, correlates findings into exposure findings with a potential attack path and blast radius diagram, so the native stack now draws attack paths within AWS.

A third-party CNAPP earns its license cost with multi-cloud coverage, agentless workload scanning, attack paths that cross cloud boundaries, Kubernetes and container depth, identity-graph correlation across clouds, and a more polished operator UX.

So if you are single-cloud AWS and disciplined, Security Hub CSPM plus Security Hub is fine. Multi-cloud, you want the third-party tool.
